  "hints": [
    "do you actually need to know every reachable index, or just the single farthest one reached so far?",
    "as you scan from left to right, what happens to your answer the moment you reach an index beyond the farthest point you've proven reachable?",
    "at each index i (as long as i is within your current reach), update the farthest reach to max(farthest, i + nums[i])",
    "if the farthest reach ever meets or passes the last index, you can stop early and return true"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "recomputing reachability from scratch at every index instead of tracking a single running 'farthest reach' value",
    "not detecting early that index i itself is unreachable, and using nums[i] anyway",
    "off-by-one when comparing the farthest reach against the last valid index"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of integers where `nums[i]` is the farthest number\nof steps you're allowed to jump forward from index i.\n\n**Starting at index 0, determine whether you're able to reach the last\nindex.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^4",
      "0 <= nums[i] <= 10^5"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-element array (already at the last index, so true)",
      "a 0 at an index that every path is forced through",
      "a jump length large enough to clear the rest of the array in one move"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
